Chapter: Simplify
  cites Codex chapter Build Settings (max-recursion-depth)

 The simplifier: one traversal that applies every local rewrite it
 knows at once, iterated to a fixpoint (GHC Core's
 one-Simplifier-pass architecture, the closest
 production analogue to this IR). This is WS-D3, slices 1 through 3. It
 carries a substitution environment down the tree and a rewrite count
 up it. It stays deliberately narrow -- no renaming, so every rewrite is
 sound by inspection: dead-let elimination and occurs-once inlining reason
 about traps and effects through an explicit purity predicate, and
 inlining refuses (rather than renames) any substitution that could
 capture a variable.

 **Constant folding.** Integer add / subtract / multiply over two
 literals, the six integer comparisons, boolean and / or, negate of a
 literal, and the three text builtins the old hardcoded pass already
 folded (text-length, char-code, char-code-at). Folding replaces an
 expression with the value the runtime would compute for it, so it is
 always sound. Division and remainder are NOT folded here: they can
 trap on a zero divisor and folding would have to preserve that trap,
 which is the next slice's concern along with the sign rules.

 **Sparse conditional constant propagation.** An `IrIf` whose condition
 has folded to a boolean literal becomes its taken arm. The untaken arm
 is never even simplified -- this is a strict language, so the arm that
 is not taken never runs, and dropping it can remove no effect and no
 trap that would otherwise have fired.

 **Literal propagation.** A `let` whose value has folded to a literal is
 dropped and every use of its name in the body is replaced by that
 literal. A literal has no free variables, so this substitution can
 capture nothing and needs no renaming; a literal is free to duplicate,
 so it is sound whether the name is used once or many times. This is the
 rewrite that carries a constant to where it can fold -- `let x = 5 in
 x + 3` becomes `8` in one traversal, because the body is simplified
 under the extended substitution before the fold runs.

 **Dead-let elimination.** A non-literal `let x = v in b` whose name x is
 never used in b, and whose value v is pure and trap-free, is dropped and
 replaced by b -- the binding and the work that computed its value both
 disappear. This is where the simplifier consumes the WS-D2 occurrence
 analyser: deadness is `occ-expr`'s free map answering that x has no free
 occurrence in b, which is correct under shadowing. It needs no renaming
 because it removes a binding rather than moving a value. The purity
 predicate is what keeps it sound in a strict language -- dropping a let
 whose value could trap or print would delete that trap or print -- so it
 fires only for values that certainly do neither (see the Purity section).

 The substitution is shadow-aware. At every binder -- a `let`, a lambda
 parameter, a match pattern variable, an act bind -- the bound name is
 removed from the substitution before its scope is walked, so an inner
 binding of a name the substitution carries hides the outer literal
 rather than being overwritten by it. Because the only values ever
 substituted are literals, removal is the whole of the discipline: there
 is no capture to rename around. The rapier -- renaming on collision so a
 non-trivial value can be substituted -- is the next slice, and this one
 is written so it never needs it.

 Two boundaries are walked with an empty substitution rather than the
 carried one: the clauses of an `IrHandle` and the three statement lists
 of an `IrTry`. A handle clause's operation and resume binders, and the
 way a try body's binds are visible in its fallback blocks, are exactly
 the unscoped-binder edges that remain unscoped. Propagating an outer
 literal across those boundaries could replace one of those leaked names.
 Refusing to carry the substitution in costs only the rare cross-boundary
 constant and keeps the slice sound without depending on those being
 closed first. The sub-expressions inside are still folded and
 propagated on their own.

 Termination. A single traversal terminates by structural recursion,
 bounded by the shared recursion depth; a subtree past the budget is
 returned unsimplified, which is always sound. The traversal is iterated
 while it reports rewrites, capped at `simplify-max-iters`. Every rewrite
 here strictly shrinks the tree or turns a name into a literal, both
 monotone, so the iteration cannot oscillate and the cap is a backstop,
 not a semantic bound; a later slice with non-monotone rewrites
 (reassociation) is where a size-proportional global budget that errors
 loudly earns its place.

 This unit is a rewrite, but it is registered as the opt-in `simplify`
 pass and is NOT in `default-ir-pipeline`. The shipping compiler's own
 compilation does not run it, so the default output is byte-identical
 and the fixed point is undisturbed; it is exercised by `-Passes
 simplify` and scored by the ablation harness. It absorbs the rules of
 the hardcoded `fold-constants` pass but does not yet replace it -- that
 swap is a codegen change to the default pipeline and its own CL.

 Limitations, stated so they are not assumed away: occurs-once inlining
 refuses rather than renames when a substitution would capture (the
 rename-on-collision rapier that would inline those cases too is a later
 refinement), so a value whose free name is bound somewhere in the body
 is left where it is; no division or remainder folding; no
 case-of-known-constructor, if-of-if commuting, reassociation, or
 strength reduction; the substitution is an
 association list scanned linearly, sized for the binders one definition
 carries; and heap is bounded by the iteration cap rather than reclaimed
 between iterations -- every iteration rebuilds the definition body on the
 pass deck, so peak is at most `simplify-max-iters` copies of the largest
 body, paid only when the pass is switched on.

Section: Results

 A walk returns the rewritten expression and the number of rewrites it
 performed, exactly as the occurrence analyser returns a free map and a
 binder list: the count threads up so the iteration driver knows whether
 a fixpoint was reached, and so the pass can report how much it did.

  SimpRes = record {
   expr : IRExpr,
   ticks : Integer
  }

 A fold either fired or it did not. Returning the outcome as a flag
 beside the expression keeps the tick accounting honest -- the caller
 adds one only when `folded` is true -- without reaching for a Maybe.

  FoldRes = record {
   folded : Boolean,
   expr : IRExpr
  }

  SimpDefRes = record {
   def : IRDef,
   ticks : Integer
  }

  SimpDefsRes = record {
   defs : List IRDef,
   ticks : Integer
  }

  SimpStmtsRes = record {
   stmts : List IRActStmt,
   ticks : Integer
  }

 What the `simplify` pass hands back to the pipeline: the rewritten
 chapter and one info line reporting the rewrite count. The infos ride
 the same channel `occ-report` uses, so the number is visible in the
 compile log and to the ablation harness without a rewrite to the
 pipeline result shape.

  SimplifyOutcome = record {
   chapter : IRChapter,
   infos : List Text
  }

  simplify-max-iters : Integer = 4

Section: Substitution

 A map from a name to the literal that replaces it. Only literals are
 ever stored, which is the property that makes propagation capture-free.
 Adding a name removes any earlier binding of it first, so at most one
 entry per name survives and it is always the innermost -- a lookup never
 has to reason about order.

  SubstEntry = record {
   name : Text,
   val : IRExpr
  }

  subst-find : List SubstEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  subst-find (m) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at m i).name == n then i
   else subst-find m n (i + 1) len

  subst-remove : List SubstEntry, Text -> List SubstEntry
  subst-remove (m) (n) = subst-remove-loop [] m n 0 (list-length m)

  subst-remove-loop : List SubstEntry, List SubstEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> List SubstEntry
  subst-remove-loop (acc) (m) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let e = list-at m i
   in subst-remove-loop (if (e.name) == n then acc else list-push acc e) m n (i + 1) len

  subst-add : List SubstEntry, Text, IRExpr -> List SubstEntry
  subst-add (m) (n) (v) = list-push (subst-remove m n) (SubstEntry { name = n, val = v })

Section: Literals

 A literal is an integer, number, text, boolean, or character constant --
 the values that are free to duplicate and cannot capture. Only these
 are substituted and only these drive the let-dropping rewrite.

  simp-is-literal : IRExpr -> Boolean
  simp-is-literal (e) =
   when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

Section: Folding

 Every fold answers with `folded = False` and the rebuilt node when no
 rule applies, so the caller rebuilds once and counts a tick only on a
 real rewrite.

  fold-none : IRExpr -> FoldRes
  fold-none (e) = FoldRes { folded = False, expr = e }

  fold-some : IRExpr -> FoldRes
  fold-some (e) = FoldRes { folded = True, expr = e }

 Integer arithmetic and comparison over two integer literals. The
 compiler runs on the same 64-bit two's-complement integers the emitted
 code uses, so the folded value is the value the program would compute,
 wrap for wrap. Division and remainder are absent by intent.

  simp-fold-int : IRBinaryOp, Integer, Integer, CodexType, SourceSpan -> FoldRes
  simp-fold-int (op) (a) (b) (ty) (sp) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt -> fold-some (deck-record (IrIntLit (a + b) sp))
    is IrSubInt -> fold-some (deck-record (IrIntLit (a - b) sp))
    is IrMulInt -> fold-some (deck-record (IrIntLit (a * b) sp))
    is IrEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a == b) sp))
    is IrNotEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a /= b) sp))
    is IrLt -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a < b) sp))
    is IrGt -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a > b) sp))
    is IrLtEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a <= b) sp))
    is IrGtEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a >= b) sp))
    is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op (deck-record (IrIntLit a sp)) (deck-record (IrIntLit b sp)) ty sp))

  simp-fold-bool : IRBinaryOp, Boolean, Boolean, CodexType, SourceSpan -> FoldRes
  simp-fold-bool (op) (a) (b) (ty) (sp) =
   when op
    is IrAnd -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a & b) sp))
    is IrOr -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a | b) sp))
    is IrEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a == b) sp))
    is IrNotEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a /= b) sp))
    is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op (deck-record (IrBoolLit a sp)) (deck-record (IrBoolLit b sp)) ty sp))

 A binary node whose operands are already simplified. When both are the
 right kind of literal a fold may fire; otherwise the node is rebuilt
 unchanged. Character equality compares code points; character ordering
 is left to a later slice.

  simp-fold-binary : IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, CodexType, SourceSpan -> FoldRes
  simp-fold-binary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) =
   when l
    is IrIntLit (a) (ls) ->
     (when r
      is IrIntLit (b) (rs) -> simp-fold-int op a b ty sp
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op l r ty sp)))
    is IrBoolLit (a) (ls) ->
     (when r
      is IrBoolLit (b) (rs) -> simp-fold-bool op a b ty sp
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op l r ty sp)))
    is IrCharLit (a) (ls) ->
     (when r
      is IrCharLit (b) (rs) ->
       (when op
        is IrEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a == b) sp))
        is IrNotEq -> fold-some (deck-record (IrBoolLit (a /= b) sp))
        is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op l r ty sp)))
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op l r ty sp)))
    is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrBinary op l r ty sp))

 The text builtins the old pass folded, recognised on already-simplified
 operands: `text-length` and `char-code` are one-argument applications,
 `char-code-at` a two-argument one whose partial application is itself an
 `IrApply`. An out-of-range index is left as a call, to trap at runtime
 exactly as it would unfolded.

 These three rules fold a name, so they must first ask whether the chapter
 DEFINES that name -- a chapter is allowed to define its own
 `text-length`. Folding one that does gave two answers in one function:
 the fold caught the literal argument and answered the builtin's way,
 while every other call site reached `emit-compound`, which checks
 `user-arity < 0` before routing to `emit-builtin` and answered the
 definition's way. `fold-constants` (Lowering,
 Section: Constant Folding) had the identical defect.

 Both are fixed the same way and the set is the same set: `dn`, a
 `SkipListText` of the chapter's own def names, built once per chapter at
 `simplify-chapter` / `simplify-run` and threaded down the walk. A name in
 it is never folded.

 `dn` is threaded rather than rebuilt per definition because the walk is
 iterated to a fixpoint -- `simplify-iter` runs the whole traversal again
 for each rewrite round, and rebuilding a skip list of every def name on
 each round would put a chapter-sized structure inside the fixpoint loop.
 It is chapter-scoped data and it is built where the chapter is.

  simp-fold-apply : SkipListText, IRExpr, IRExpr, CodexType, SourceSpan -> FoldRes
  simp-fold-apply (dn) (f) (a) (ty) (sp) =
   when f
    is IrName (n) (fty) (ns) ->
     if skip-list-text-has dn n then fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp))
     else if n == "text-length" then (when a
      is IrTextLit (s) (as) -> fold-some (deck-record (IrIntLit (text-length s) sp))
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp)))
     else if n == "char-code" then (when a
      is IrCharLit (v) (as) -> fold-some (deck-record (IrIntLit v sp))
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp)))
     else fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp))
    is IrApply (inner-f) (inner-a) (fty) (fs) ->
     (when inner-f
      is IrName (n) (nty) (nsp) ->
       if n == "char-code-at" then (if skip-list-text-has dn n then fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp))
        else simp-fold-char-code-at f inner-a a ty sp)
       else fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp))
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp)))
    is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrApply f a ty sp))

  simp-fold-char-code-at : IRExpr, IRExpr, IRExpr, CodexType, SourceSpan -> FoldRes
  simp-fold-char-code-at (partial) (str-arg) (idx-arg) (ty) (sp) =
   when str-arg
    is IrTextLit (s) (as) ->
     (when idx-arg
      is IrIntLit (i) (is2) ->
       if i >= 0 then (if i < text-length s then fold-some (deck-record (IrIntLit (char-code-at s i) sp))
        else fold-none (deck-record (IrApply partial idx-arg ty sp)))
       else fold-none (deck-record (IrApply partial idx-arg ty sp))
      is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrApply partial idx-arg ty sp)))
    is otherwise -> fold-none (deck-record (IrApply partial idx-arg ty sp))

Section: Purity and Deadness

 Dead-let elimination drops `let x = v in b` when x is never used in b
 and evaluating v has no observable consequence. Both halves are needed:
 an unused binding whose value could trap or print must not be dropped,
 because dropping it deletes the trap or the print. This is where the
 simplifier consumes the occurrence analyser (`IR/Occurrence.codex`,
 WS-D2): deadness is read from `occ-expr`'s free map, which counts free
 names with correct shadowing, so a name shadowed by an inner binder of
 the same name is not mistaken for a use of the outer one -- the exact
 hazard a flat name-keyed set would fall into.

 The purity predicate is conservative in the safe direction: it answers
 True only for expressions that certainly cannot trap and certainly have
 no effect, so a False merely keeps a let that could have been dropped.
 Literals, names (referencing a value neither traps nor acts), the
 non-trapping integer / boolean / text operators, and the pure structural
 forms (if, let, lambda, list, record, field access) qualify. Division
 and remainder trap on a zero divisor; application can call anything; a
 match can be inexhaustive; act, handle, try, fork, await, field store,
 and error carry effects or traps. None of those qualify, so a let
 binding one of them is kept even when its name is dead.

  simp-op-trapfree : IRBinaryOp -> Boolean
  simp-op-trapfree (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt | IrSubInt | IrMulInt | IrEq | IrNotEq | IrLt | IrGt | IrLtEq | IrGtEq | IrAnd | IrOr | IrAppendText | IrAppendList | IrConsList -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  simp-pure-trapfree : IRExpr, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-pure-trapfree (e) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then False
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> True
    is IrName (nm) (t) (s) -> True
    is IrNegate (x) (t) (s) -> simp-pure-trapfree x (depth + 1)
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     if simp-op-trapfree op then (if simp-pure-trapfree l (depth + 1) then simp-pure-trapfree r (depth + 1) else False) else False
    is IrIf (c) (th) (el) (t) (s) ->
     if simp-pure-trapfree c (depth + 1) then (if simp-pure-trapfree th (depth + 1) then simp-pure-trapfree el (depth + 1) else False) else False
    is IrLet (nm) (t) (v) (bd) (s) ->
     if simp-pure-trapfree v (depth + 1) then simp-pure-trapfree bd (depth + 1) else False
    is IrLambda (ps) (bd) (t) (s) -> True
    is IrList (es) (t) (s) -> simp-pure-trapfree-list es (depth + 1) 0 (list-length es)
    is IrRecord (nm) (fs) (t) (s) -> simp-pure-trapfree-fields fs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fs)
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (s) -> simp-pure-trapfree r (depth + 1)
    is otherwise -> False

  simp-pure-trapfree-list : List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-pure-trapfree-list (es) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then True
   else if simp-pure-trapfree (list-at es i) depth then simp-pure-trapfree-list es depth (i + 1) len
   else False

  simp-pure-trapfree-fields : List IRFieldVal, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-pure-trapfree-fields (fs) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then True
   else if simp-pure-trapfree ((list-at fs i).value) depth then simp-pure-trapfree-fields fs depth (i + 1) len
   else False

 A name is dead in a body when the occurrence analyser's free map
 classifies it Dead -- absent from the map, because no free reference to
 it survives the body's binders.

  simp-is-dead : Text, IRExpr -> Boolean
  simp-is-dead (n) (b) =
   when occ-map-get ((occ-expr b [] 0).free) n
    is OccDead -> True
    is otherwise -> False

Section: Inlining

 A let bound to a non-literal value may still be inlined -- its name
 replaced by the value and the binding dropped -- in two cases, and only
 when doing so cannot capture. A value bound to a bare name is
 copy-propagated at any occurrence count: a name is pure, trap-free, and
 free to duplicate, so replacing every use costs nothing and can change
 nothing. A value that is pure and trap-free and whose name occurs
 exactly once is inlined into that one use: no work is duplicated because
 there is one use, and the value cannot trap or act, so moving where it
 is evaluated is unobservable -- the property that makes this sound in a
 strict language, where a conditional use would otherwise decide whether
 the value's trap fires.

 Capture is refused, not renamed. Substituting a value that carries free
 variables into a body that binds one of those names would rebind the
 value's variable to the wrong thing. This slice detects that -- does any
 binder in the body carry a free name of the value -- and declines to
 inline when it could happen, keeping the let. Declining is strictly
 sound; the alternative, renaming the colliding binder to a fresh name
 (the rapier), is more complete and is a later refinement. Because the
 fixed point cannot see a capture miscompile -- a wrongly captured program
 still compiles to a consistent wrong answer -- the guard here is the one
 that matters, and the adversarial `sc-cap` fixture pins it.

 The occurrence count and the value's free names both come from the WS-D2
 occurrence analyser's free map, so shadowing is handled the same way the
 rest of the simplifier handles it.

  simp-free-names : IRExpr -> List Text
  simp-free-names (e) = for en in ((occ-expr e [] 0).free) -> en.name

  simp-occ-once : Text, IRExpr -> Boolean
  simp-occ-once (n) (b) =
   when occ-map-get ((occ-expr b [] 0).free) n
    is OccOnce -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  simp-name-in : List Text, Text -> Boolean
  simp-name-in (names) (n) = simp-name-in-loop names n 0 (list-length names)

  simp-name-in-loop : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-name-in-loop (names) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if list-at names i == n then True
   else simp-name-in-loop names n (i + 1) len

 Does any binder anywhere in the expression carry a name in the set? A
 True here forbids an inline that would otherwise capture. The walk is
 depth-bounded, and an exhausted budget answers True -- the conservative
 direction, since an unknown body might bind a captured name.

  simp-binds-any : IRExpr, List Text, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-binds-any (e) (names) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then True
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> False
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) -> False
    is IrError (m) (t) (s) -> False
    is IrNegate (x) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any x names (depth + 1)
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any l names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any r names (depth + 1)
    is IrIf (c) (th) (el) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any c names (depth + 1) then True else if simp-binds-any th names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any el names (depth + 1)
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (s) -> if simp-name-in names n then True else if simp-binds-any v names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any b names (depth + 1)
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any f names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any a names (depth + 1)
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (t) (s) -> if simp-params-in names ps 0 (list-length ps) then True else simp-binds-any b names (depth + 1)
    is IrList (es) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any-list es names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length es)
    is IrMatch (sc) (bs) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any sc names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any-branches bs names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length bs)
    is IrAct (ss) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any-stmts ss names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length ss)
    is IrHandle (eff) (h) (cs) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any h names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any-clauses cs names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length cs)
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any body names (depth + 1)
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any-fields fs names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fs)
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any r names (depth + 1)
    is IrFork (b) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any b names (depth + 1)
    is IrAwait (tk) (t) (s) -> simp-binds-any tk names (depth + 1)
    is IrTry (mx) (body) (fb) (fail) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any-stmts body names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length body) then True else if simp-binds-any-stmts fb names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fb) then True else simp-binds-any-stmts fail names (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fail)
    is IrFieldStore (r) (f) (v) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any r names (depth + 1) then True else simp-binds-any v names (depth + 1)

  simp-params-in : List Text, List IRParam, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-params-in (names) (ps) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if simp-name-in names ((list-at ps i).name) then True
   else simp-params-in names ps (i + 1) len

  simp-binds-any-list : List IRExpr, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-binds-any-list (es) (names) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if simp-binds-any (list-at es i) names depth then True
   else simp-binds-any-list es names depth (i + 1) len

  simp-binds-any-fields : List IRFieldVal, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-binds-any-fields (fs) (names) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if simp-binds-any ((list-at fs i).value) names depth then True
   else simp-binds-any-fields fs names depth (i + 1) len

 A branch binds its pattern's variables over the guard and body, so a
 pattern variable in the set is itself a capture; otherwise the guard and
 body are walked.

  simp-binds-any-branches : List IRBranch, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-binds-any-branches (bs) (names) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in if simp-pat-binds names (b.pattern) (depth + 1) then True
      else if simp-binds-any (b.guard) names depth then True
      else if simp-binds-any (b.body) names depth then True
      else simp-binds-any-branches bs names depth (i + 1) len

  simp-pat-binds : List Text, IRPat, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-pat-binds (names) (p) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then True
   else when p
    is IrVarPat (n) (t) (s) -> simp-name-in names n
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (t) (s) -> simp-pat-binds-list names subs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is IrVecPat (subs) (t) (s) -> simp-pat-binds-list names subs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is otherwise -> False

  simp-pat-binds-list : List Text, List IRPat, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-pat-binds-list (names) (ps) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if simp-pat-binds names (list-at ps i) depth then True
   else simp-pat-binds-list names ps depth (i + 1) len

 An act statement's bind, in either an `IrAct` or the statement lists of
 an `IrTry`, is a binder over the statements after it.

  simp-binds-any-stmts : List IRActStmt, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-binds-any-stmts (ss) (names) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else let s = list-at ss i
   in when s
    is IrDoExec (v) (sp) -> if simp-binds-any v names depth then True else simp-binds-any-stmts ss names depth (i + 1) len
    is IrDoBind (n) (t) (v) (sp) -> if simp-name-in names n then True else if simp-binds-any v names depth then True else simp-binds-any-stmts ss names depth (i + 1) len

  simp-binds-any-clauses : List IRHandleClause, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-binds-any-clauses (cs) (names) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else let c = list-at cs i
   in if simp-name-in names (c.resume-name) then True
      else if simp-name-in-list names (c.params) 0 (list-length (c.params)) then True
      else if simp-binds-any (c.body) names depth then True
      else simp-binds-any-clauses cs names depth (i + 1) len

  simp-name-in-list : List Text, List Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  simp-name-in-list (names) (xs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if simp-name-in names (list-at xs i) then True
   else simp-name-in-list names xs (i + 1) len

 The admission test. A bare name is copy-propagated whenever the body
 does not bind it. Any other value must be pure and trap-free, occur
 exactly once, and carry no free name the body binds.

  simp-inline-ok : Text, IRExpr, IRExpr -> Boolean
  simp-inline-ok (n) (v) (b) =
   when v
    is IrName (y) (t) (s) -> if simp-binds-any b [y] 0 then False else True
    is otherwise ->
     if simp-pure-trapfree v 0 then
      (if simp-occ-once n b then (if simp-binds-any b (simp-free-names v) 0 then False else True) else False)
     else False

Section: Expression Walk

 One traversal, carrying the substitution down and the rewrite count up.
 A node past the depth budget is returned untouched -- sound, because
 leaving a subtree unsimplified never changes its meaning.

  simplify-expr : SkipListText, List SubstEntry, IRExpr, Integer -> SimpRes
  simplify-expr (dn) (env) (e) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then SimpRes { expr = e, ticks = 0 }
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> SimpRes { expr = e, ticks = 0 }
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) ->
     let i = subst-find env n 0 (list-length env)
     in if i < 0 then SimpRes { expr = e, ticks = 0 }
        else SimpRes { expr = (list-at env i).val, ticks = 1 }
    is IrNegate (x) (t) (s) ->
     let rx = simplify-expr dn env x (depth + 1)
     in (when rx.expr
      is IrIntLit (v) (ls) -> SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrIntLit (-v) s), ticks = rx.ticks + 1 }
      is otherwise -> SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrNegate (rx.expr) t s), ticks = rx.ticks })
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     let rl = simplify-expr dn env l (depth + 1)
     in let rr = simplify-expr dn env r (depth + 1)
     in let fr = simp-fold-binary op (rl.expr) (rr.expr) t s
     in SimpRes { expr = fr.expr, ticks = rl.ticks + rr.ticks + (if fr.folded then 1 else 0) }
    is IrIf (c) (th) (el) (t) (s) ->
     let rc = simplify-expr dn env c (depth + 1)
     in (when rc.expr
      is IrBoolLit (b) (cs) ->
       let rt = simplify-expr dn env (if b then th else el) (depth + 1)
       in SimpRes { expr = rt.expr, ticks = rc.ticks + rt.ticks + 1 }
      is otherwise ->
       let rt = simplify-expr dn env th (depth + 1)
       in let re = simplify-expr dn env el (depth + 1)
       in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrIf (rc.expr) (rt.expr) (re.expr) t s), ticks = rc.ticks + rt.ticks + re.ticks })
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (s) ->
     let rv = simplify-expr dn env v (depth + 1)
     in if simp-is-literal (rv.expr)
        then let rb = simplify-expr (dn) (subst-add env n (rv.expr)) b (depth + 1)
             in SimpRes { expr = rb.expr, ticks = rv.ticks + rb.ticks + 1 }
        else if simp-inline-ok n (rv.expr) b
        then let rb = simplify-expr (dn) (subst-add env n (rv.expr)) b (depth + 1)
             in SimpRes { expr = rb.expr, ticks = rv.ticks + rb.ticks + 1 }
        else let rb = simplify-expr (dn) (subst-remove env n) b (depth + 1)
             in if simp-pure-trapfree (rv.expr) 0 & simp-is-dead n (rb.expr)
                then SimpRes { expr = rb.expr, ticks = rv.ticks + rb.ticks + 1 }
                else SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrLet n t (rv.expr) (rb.expr) s), ticks = rv.ticks + rb.ticks }
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) ->
     let rf = simplify-expr dn env f (depth + 1)
     in let ra = simplify-expr dn env a (depth + 1)
     in let fr = simp-fold-apply dn (rf.expr) (ra.expr) t s
     in SimpRes { expr = fr.expr, ticks = rf.ticks + ra.ticks + (if fr.folded then 1 else 0) }
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (t) (s) ->
     let rb = simplify-expr (dn) (subst-remove-names env ps 0 (list-length ps)) b (depth + 1)
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrLambda ps (rb.expr) t s), ticks = rb.ticks }
    is IrList (es) (t) (s) ->
     let r = simplify-exprs dn env es (depth + 1) 0 (list-length es) [] 0
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrList (r.stmts-as-exprs) t s), ticks = r.ticks }
    is IrMatch (sc) (bs) (t) (s) ->
     let rsc = simplify-expr dn env sc (depth + 1)
     in let rb = simplify-branches dn env bs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length bs) [] 0
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrMatch (rsc.expr) (rb.branches) t s), ticks = rsc.ticks + rb.ticks }
    is IrAct (ss) (t) (s) ->
     let r = simplify-stmts dn env ss (depth + 1) 0 (list-length ss) [] 0
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrAct (r.stmts) t s), ticks = r.ticks }
    is IrHandle (eff) (h) (cs) (t) (s) ->
     let rh = simplify-expr dn env h (depth + 1)
     in let rc = simplify-clauses dn cs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length cs) [] 0
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrHandle eff (rh.expr) (rc.clauses) t s), ticks = rh.ticks + rc.ticks }
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (t) (s) ->
     let rb = simplify-expr dn env body (depth + 1)
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrWithTimeout secs effs scopes (rb.expr) t s), ticks = rb.ticks }
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (t) (s) ->
     let r = simplify-fields dn env fs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fs) [] 0
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrRecord n (r.fields) t s), ticks = r.ticks }
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (s) ->
     let rr = simplify-expr dn env r (depth + 1)
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrFieldAccess (rr.expr) f t s), ticks = rr.ticks }
    is IrFork (b) (t) (s) ->
     let rb = simplify-expr dn env b (depth + 1)
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrFork (rb.expr) t s), ticks = rb.ticks }
    is IrAwait (tk) (t) (s) ->
     let rt = simplify-expr dn env tk (depth + 1)
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrAwait (rt.expr) t s), ticks = rt.ticks }
    is IrTry (mx) (body) (fb) (fail) (t) (s) ->
     let r1 = simplify-stmts dn [] body (depth + 1) 0 (list-length body) [] 0
     in let r2 = simplify-stmts dn [] fb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fb) [] 0
     in let r3 = simplify-stmts dn [] fail (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fail) [] 0
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrTry mx (r1.stmts) (r2.stmts) (r3.stmts) t s), ticks = r1.ticks + r2.ticks + r3.ticks }
    is IrFieldStore (r) (f) (v) (t) (s) ->
     let rr = simplify-expr dn env r (depth + 1)
     in let rv = simplify-expr dn env v (depth + 1)
     in SimpRes { expr = deck-record (IrFieldStore (rr.expr) f (rv.expr) t s), ticks = rr.ticks + rv.ticks }
    is IrError (m) (t) (s) -> SimpRes { expr = e, ticks = 0 }

 Removing a run of names from the substitution: the parameters of a
 lambda, all at once, before its body is walked.

  subst-remove-names : List SubstEntry, List IRParam, Integer, Integer -> List SubstEntry
  subst-remove-names (env) (ps) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then env
   else subst-remove-names (subst-remove env ((list-at ps i).name)) ps (i + 1) len

Section: List and Field Walks

 Sibling expressions share one substitution and accumulate their ticks.
 The result reuses `SimpStmtsRes`'s free-form shape through a companion
 record so a list of expressions threads exactly like a list of
 statements does.

  SimpExprsRes = record {
   stmts-as-exprs : List IRExpr,
   ticks : Integer
  }

  simplify-exprs : SkipListText, List SubstEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer, List IRExpr, Integer -> SimpExprsRes
  simplify-exprs (dn) (env) (es) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) (ticks) =
   if i >= len then SimpExprsRes { stmts-as-exprs = acc, ticks = ticks }
   else let r = simplify-expr dn env (list-at es i) depth
   in simplify-exprs dn env es depth (i + 1) len (list-push acc (r.expr)) (ticks + r.ticks)

  SimpFieldsRes = record {
   fields : List IRFieldVal,
   ticks : Integer
  }

  simplify-fields : SkipListText, List SubstEntry, List IRFieldVal, Integer, Integer, Integer, List IRFieldVal, Integer -> SimpFieldsRes
  simplify-fields (dn) (env) (fs) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) (ticks) =
   if i >= len then SimpFieldsRes { fields = acc, ticks = ticks }
   else let f = list-at fs i
   in let r = simplify-expr dn env (f.value) depth
   in simplify-fields dn env fs depth (i + 1) len (list-push acc (deck-record (__record-set f "value" (r.expr)))) (ticks + r.ticks)

Section: Statement, Branch, and Clause Walks

 An act statement's bind is in scope for the statements after it, so its
 name is removed from the substitution before the tail is walked -- the
 same do-notation scoping the occurrence analyser and the emitter follow.
 The bind's own value is simplified under the substitution as it stands,
 because the value is evaluated in the scope before the bind.

  simplify-stmts : SkipListText, List SubstEntry, List IRActStmt, Integer, Integer, Integer, List IRActStmt, Integer -> SimpStmtsRes
  simplify-stmts (dn) (env) (ss) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) (ticks) =
   if i >= len then SimpStmtsRes { stmts = acc, ticks = ticks }
   else let s = list-at ss i
   in when s
    is IrDoExec (v) (sp) ->
     let rv = simplify-expr dn env v depth
     in simplify-stmts dn env ss depth (i + 1) len (list-push acc (deck-record (IrDoExec (rv.expr) sp))) (ticks + rv.ticks)
    is IrDoBind (n) (t) (v) (sp) ->
     let rv = simplify-expr dn env v depth
     in simplify-stmts dn (subst-remove env n) ss depth (i + 1) len (list-push acc (deck-record (IrDoBind n t (rv.expr) sp))) (ticks + rv.ticks)

 A branch pattern binds over its guard and body only. The pattern
 variables are stripped from the substitution before the branch is
 walked; the scrutinee was already simplified by the caller.

  SimpBranchesRes = record {
   branches : List IRBranch,
   ticks : Integer
  }

  simplify-branches : SkipListText, List SubstEntry, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer, Integer, List IRBranch, Integer -> SimpBranchesRes
  simplify-branches (dn) (env) (bs) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) (ticks) =
   if i >= len then SimpBranchesRes { branches = acc, ticks = ticks }
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let benv = subst-remove-pat env (b.pattern) depth
   in let rg = simplify-expr dn benv (b.guard) depth
   in let rbody = simplify-expr dn benv (b.body) depth
   in simplify-branches dn env bs depth (i + 1) len (list-push acc (deck-record (IRBranch { pattern = b.pattern, body = rbody.expr, guard = rg.expr, span = b.span, alt-group = b.alt-group }))) (ticks + rg.ticks + rbody.ticks)

  subst-remove-pat : List SubstEntry, IRPat, Integer -> List SubstEntry
  subst-remove-pat (env) (p) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then env
   else when p
    is IrVarPat (n) (t) (s) -> subst-remove env n
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (t) (s) -> subst-remove-pats env subs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is IrVecPat (subs) (t) (s) -> subst-remove-pats env subs (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is otherwise -> env

  subst-remove-pats : List SubstEntry, List IRPat, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List SubstEntry
  subst-remove-pats (env) (ps) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then env
   else subst-remove-pats (subst-remove-pat env (list-at ps i) depth) ps depth (i + 1) len

 Handle clauses are walked under an empty substitution -- see the chapter
 prose for why the operation, resume, and leaked try binders make
 carrying the outer substitution in unsound until those are scoped.

  SimpClausesRes = record {
   clauses : List IRHandleClause,
   ticks : Integer
  }

  simplify-clauses : SkipListText, List IRHandleClause, Integer, Integer, Integer, List IRHandleClause, Integer -> SimpClausesRes
  simplify-clauses (dn) (cs) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) (ticks) =
   if i >= len then SimpClausesRes { clauses = acc, ticks = ticks }
   else let c = list-at cs i
   in let rb = simplify-expr dn [] (c.body) depth
   in simplify-clauses dn cs depth (i + 1) len (list-push acc (deck-record (__record-set c "body" (rb.expr)))) (ticks + rb.ticks)

Section: Definition and Chapter

 A definition is simplified by iterating one traversal of its body until
 a traversal reports no rewrites or the cap is reached. The substitution
 starts empty at the definition boundary -- a parameter is not a literal,
 so nothing propagates in from the signature.

  simplify-def : SkipListText, IRDef -> SimpDefRes
  simplify-def (dn) (d) =
   let r = simplify-iter dn (d.body) 0 0
   in let d2 = __record-set d "body" (r.expr)
   in SimpDefRes { def = deck-record d2, ticks = r.ticks }

  simplify-iter : SkipListText, IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> SimpRes
  simplify-iter (dn) (body) (iter) (acc) =
   let r = simplify-expr dn [] body 0
   in if r.ticks == 0 then SimpRes { expr = r.expr, ticks = acc }
      else if (iter + 1) >= simplify-max-iters then SimpRes { expr = r.expr, ticks = acc + r.ticks }
      else simplify-iter dn (r.expr) (iter + 1) (acc + r.ticks)

  simplify-defs : SkipListText, List IRDef, Integer, Integer, List IRDef, Integer -> SimpDefsRes
  simplify-defs (dn) (defs) (i) (len) (acc) (ticks) =
   if i >= len then SimpDefsRes { defs = acc, ticks = ticks }
   else let r = simplify-def dn (list-at defs i)
   in simplify-defs dn defs (i + 1) len (list-push acc (r.def)) (ticks + r.ticks)

 The pure rewrite, for the day the simplifier replaces `fold-constants`
 in the default pipeline: chapter in, chapter out, no report.

  simplify-chapter : IRChapter -> IRChapter
  simplify-chapter (ch) =
   let dn = deck-record (skip-list-text-from-list (for d in (ch.defs) -> d.name))
   in __record-set ch "defs" ((simplify-defs dn (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs)) [] 0).defs)

 The opt-in pass: the same rewrite, plus one info line carrying the
 rewrite count for the compile log and the ablation harness.

  simplify-run : IRChapter -> SimplifyOutcome
  simplify-run (ch) =
   let dn = deck-record (skip-list-text-from-list (for d in (ch.defs) -> d.name))
   in let r = simplify-defs dn (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs)) [] 0
   in let ch2 = __record-set ch "defs" (r.defs)
   in SimplifyOutcome {
    chapter = ch2,
    infos = [ "SIMPLIFY: rewrites=" & integer-to-text (r.ticks) ]
   }
